2026-05-05 22:30:27.360 INFO  - INPUT  {technique_id=T0877, technique_name=Exfiltration, technique_description=Adversaries may transfer data out of an ICS network to accomplish their objectives., control_name=Network-based firewalls, control_description=Network-based firewalls are dedicated network-mounted devices that inspect and filter network traffic. Their implementations range from simple stateless firewalls, which filter traffic strictly based on a static set of conditions (Source/Destination, port, protocol), through stateful and protocol-aware firewalls, to next-generation firewalls (NGFW) that can also realise application-aware filtering and/or intrusion detection features (which are covered under a separate control). Dedicated firewall implementations also exist for common ICS protocols. In the context of ICS, the primary purpose of a network-based firewall is to implement network access control, which restricts acceptable traffic for each source and destination, and traffic filtering, which ensures that malicious communication patterns do not get routed to destination. This can take either form of allowlists, for when the acceptable forms of traffic can be exhaustively defined, or denylists, which are useful when not all acceptable traffic can be defined, but various types of unacceptable traffic can. In addition, firewalls are a common method of establishing logical network segmentation. A common notable example of this is separating OT networks from the wider IT environment by restricting traffic between them.}
2026-05-05 22:30:32.197 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":3,"coverage_justification":"Network-based firewalls provide moderate effectiveness against data exfiltration in ICS environments through several mechanisms: (1) They can enforce egress filtering policies that restrict outbound traffic to only authorized destinations and protocols, directly impeding unauthorized data transfer attempts; (2) They enable network segmentation that limits which systems can communicate externally, reducing the attack surface for exfiltration; (3) Protocol-aware firewalls can detect anomalous data flows that deviate from normal operational patterns. However, the control has significant limitations: (1) Firewalls primarily operate at network layers 3-4 and basic application awareness, so encrypted tunnels or legitimate-looking protocols can bypass detection; (2) They cannot detect exfiltration via legitimate outbound channels if policies permit such traffic (e.g., DNS queries, HTTP/HTTPS); (3) They do not address insider threats or compromised systems with legitimate access credentials; (4) Protocol-aware firewalls for ICS are less mature than IT counterparts, and many OT environments lack them; (5) Firewalls cannot prevent data exfiltration through air-gapped systems or physical media removal. In a typical OT environment with properly configured egress filtering and network segmentation, firewalls can reduce exfiltration impact by preventing 50-75% of common attack paths (blocking unauthorized external destinations, restricting lateral movement to internet-facing systems), but sophisticated adversaries can still exfiltrate data through encrypted channels, legitimate protocols, or by moving data incrementally across segmented boundaries. The control is most effective when combined with data loss prevention (DLP) and advanced monitoring capabilities."}
2026-05-06 07:33:05.679 INFO  - INPUT  {technique_id=T0886, technique_name=Remote Services, technique_description=Adversaries may attempt to leverage or create a trusted relationship between networks to move laterally or gain access to restricted networks., control_name=Network-based firewalls, control_description=Network-based firewalls are dedicated network-mounted devices that inspect and filter network traffic. Their implementations range from simple stateless firewalls, which filter traffic strictly based on a static set of conditions (Source/Destination, port, protocol), through stateful and protocol-aware firewalls, to next-generation firewalls (NGFW) that can also realise application-aware filtering and/or intrusion detection features (which are covered under a separate control). Dedicated firewall implementations also exist for common ICS protocols. In the context of ICS, the primary purpose of a network-based firewall is to implement network access control, which restricts acceptable traffic for each source and destination, and traffic filtering, which ensures that malicious communication patterns do not get routed to destination. This can take either form of allowlists, for when the acceptable forms of traffic can be exhaustively defined, or denylists, which are useful when not all acceptable traffic can be defined, but various types of unacceptable traffic can. In addition, firewalls are a common method of establishing logical network segmentation. A common notable example of this is separating OT networks from the wider IT environment by restricting traffic between them.}
2026-05-06 07:33:10.568 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":4,"coverage_justification":"Network-based firewalls provide substantial protection against T0886 (Remote Services) by implementing network access control policies that restrict which systems can establish remote connections and from where. The control directly addresses the technique's core mechanism of leveraging trusted relationships through several key capabilities: (1) Stateful inspection can identify and block unauthorized remote service connections that deviate from established baseline traffic patterns; (2) Protocol-aware filtering can detect and prevent exploitation of legitimate remote protocols (RDP, SSH, VPN, etc.) when configured with strict allowlists; (3) Network segmentation via firewalls creates logical boundaries that prevent lateral movement between OT and IT networks, limiting an attacker's ability to pivot after compromising a remote service; (4) Application-aware NGFW implementations can enforce granular controls based on remote service behavior and patterns. However, the control does not fully achieve a rating of 5 because: firewalls cannot prevent attacks that originate from within already-trusted networks or from compromised systems that legitimately have remote service access; they cannot detect all variants of remote service abuse if attackers use encrypted channels or legitimate protocols configured for unauthorized purposes; and they do not address the initial compromise of a system that becomes the pivot point for lateral movement. Additionally, in ICS environments with legacy systems requiring broad remote access for legitimate maintenance, firewall rules may be relaxed to accommodate operational needs, reducing effectiveness. The control reduces blast radius by 75-90% through proper enforcement of network access policies and segmentation, but some risk remains from insider threats and sophisticated attacks using legitimate remote service channels."}
